 Some results on total weight choosability // Restore the saved color scheme preference, or // enact the browser preference if "automatic", // without expecting DOM load to have completed. // // Also restore any saved readingmode and ToC display preferences. function initializeReadingPreferences() { let saved_theme = localStorage.getItem("ar5iv_theme") || "automatic"; if (saved_theme === "automatic") { if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches) { saved_theme = "dark"; } } if (saved_theme == "dark") { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "dark"); } else { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "light"); } const tocDisplay = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_toc_display'); if (tocDisplay) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-toc-display", tocDisplay); } const readingMode = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_reading_mode'); if (readingMode) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-reading-mode", readingMode); } } // Run as soon as JS starts, to minimize repainting initializeReadingPreferences(); /* Reading mode hides the chrome and the empty .arxiv-html-header marker: the pinned JS reads the marker's display to know which way to toggle. The marker draws no box. */ .arxiv-html-header { display: contents; } html[data-reading-mode="enabled"] :is(.arxiv-html-site-chrome, .arxiv-html-header, .ds-site-footer) { display: none; } /* The paper controls' icons are blocks, so they sit centred (the pinned JS shows the theme icon by making its span a block). */ .arxiv-html-site-chrome .ds-site-header-tools svg { display: block; width: 18px; height: 18px; flex-shrink: 0; fill: currentColor; } /* ar5iv dims every image in dark mode and puts it on a white wash, for paper figures; arXiv's own render as they are (the logo on the bar, the funder logos on their white chips). Important: ar5iv's dark rules are unlayered, as specific as :root:not([data-theme]) img:not(.ltx_no_dark_filter). */ :is(.ds-site-header, .ds-site-footer) img { filter: none !important; } .ds-site-header img { background-color: transparent !important; } /* Wide (the theme's 1280px): the theme lays the body out as a grid whose full-width top rows were the band's and its sticky reader bar's. The band takes the first and scrolls away; the header, the paper's bar (no site navigation, the paper's controls), takes the second and stays in view, the one sticky bar, with the anchor clearance that requires (DESIGN-POLICIES). The controls show their names. */ @media (min-width: 1280px) { html { scroll-padding-top: 80px; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome { display: contents; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > #ds-announcement { grid-area: announce; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > .ds-site-header { grid-area: header; position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 3; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-icon { display: none; } } /* Narrow: the header scrolls away, and the controls show icons (names kept for screen readers; Why HTML? goes). The theme's contents panel is fixed below where its old sticky bar was, and hidden only once the reader has closed it, so until then it is an invisible box over the header and the paper: it shows only while open, under the chrome, in its place in the page. The paper's footer (build logs, reporting instructions) keeps the text's side inset; the theme gives it none, so it met the screen edges. */ @media (max-width: 1279px) { .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-wide { display: none; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-label { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; } html:not([data-toc-display="block"]) .ltx_page_navbar { display: none; } .ltx_page_navbar { position: absolute; top: auto; } .arxiv-html-footer { padding-inline: 1rem; } } Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Submit without GitHub Submit in GitHub Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme 1 Introduction 2 A solution to Problem 3 Total weight choosability of ( n , m ) (n,m) -graphs when m = n m=n and n + 1 n+1 4 Total weight choosability of some graphs under some graph decorations References License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2403.01492v1 [math.CO] 03 Mar 2024 Some results on total weight choosability Thanks: supported by NSFC (No. 12261071), NSF of Qinghai Province (No. 2020-ZJ-920), NSFC Grant (No. 11901263, 12071194, 12271228) and NSFC of Gansu Province (No. 21JR7RA511). Tingzeng Wu † † thanks: Corresponding author. E-mail address : mathtzwu@163.com, jianxuanluo@163.com, gaoyp@lzu.edu.cn Affiliation: School of Mathematics and Statistics, Qinghai Nationalities University, Xining, Qinghai 810007, P.R. China Affiliation: Qinghai Institute of Applied Mathematics, Xining, Qinghai, 810007, P.R. China Jianxuan Luo Affiliation: School of Mathematics and Statistics, Qinghai Nationalities University, Xining, Qinghai 810007, P.R. China Yuping Gao Affiliation: School of Mathematics and Statistics, Lanzhou University,Lanzhou, Gansu, 730000, P.R. China Abstract: A graph G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) is called ( k , k ′ ) (k,k^{\prime}) -choosable if for any total list assignment L L which assigns to each vertex v v a set L ⁡ ( v ) L(v) of k k real numbers, and assigns to each edge e e a set L ⁡ ( e ) L(e) of k ′ k^{\prime} real numbers, there is a mapping f : V ∪ E → ℝ f:V\cup E\rightarrow\mathbb{R} such that f ⁡ ( y ) ∈ L ⁡ ( y ) f(y)\in L(y) for any y ∈ V ∪ E y\in V\cup E and for any two adjacent vertices v , v ′ v,v^{\prime} , ∑ e ∈ E ⁡ ( v ) f ⁡ ( e ) + f ⁡ ( v ) ≠ ∑ e ∈ E ⁡ ( v ′ ) f ⁡ ( e ) + f ⁡ ( v ′ ) \sum_{e\in E(v)}f(e)+f(v)\neq\sum_{e\in E(v^{\prime})}f(e)+f(v^{\prime}) , where E ⁡ ( x ) E(x) denotes the set of incident edges of a vertex x ∈ V ⁡ ( G ) x\in V(G) . In this paper, we characterize a sufficient condition on ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable of graphs. We show that every connected ( n , m ) (n,m) -graph is both ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) -choosable and ( 1 , 3 ) (1,3) -choosable if m = n m=n or n + 1 n+1 , where ( n , m ) (n,m) -graph denotes the graph with n n vertices and m m edges. Furthermore, we prove that some graphs obtained by some graph operations are ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) -choosable. Keywords: Total-weighting; List assignment; Permanent index; Line graph; Graph operation 1 Introduction Let G = ( V ⁡ ( G ) , E ⁡ ( G ) ) G=(V(G),E(G)) be a graph with n n vertices. The number of edges in G G is denoted by m ⁡ ( G ) m(G) or m m for short. We also call G G as an ( n , m ) (n,m) - graph . For a subgraph H H of G G , let G − E ⁡ ( H ) G-E(H) denotes the subgraph obtained from G G by deleting the edges of H H . A matching in a graph is a set of non-loop edges with no common endvertices, and an endvertex in an edge of a matching is said to be saturated by the matching. A perfect matching in a graph is a matching that saturates every vertex. The number of perfect matchings of G G is denoted by M ⁡ ( G ) M(G) . For convenience, a path, a cycle and a complete graph with n n vertices are denoted by P n P_{n} , C n C_{n} and K n K_{n} , respectively. A total-weighting of a graph G G is a mapping f f : V ∪ E → ℝ V\cup E\rightarrow\mathbb{R} which assigns to each vertex and each edge a real number as its weight. For a total-weighting f f , we use s ⁡ ( v ) = f ⁡ ( v ) + ∑ e ∈ E ⁡ ( v ) f ⁡ ( e ) s(v)=f(v)+\sum_{e\in E(v)}f(e) to denote the weight of a vertex v ∈ V ⁡ ( G ) v\in V(G) , where E ⁡ ( v ) E(v) denotes the set of edges incident with v v . If s ⁡ ( u ) ≠ s ⁡ ( v ) s(u)\neq s(v) for any two adjacent vertices u , v ∈ V ⁡ ( G ) u,v\in V(G) , then we call the total-weighting f f proper . The list version of total-weighting of graphs was introduced independently by Przybyło and Woźniak [ 6 ] and Wong and Zhu [ 8 ] . Let ψ : V ∪ E → ℕ + \psi:V\cup E\rightarrow\mathbb{N}^{+} . A ψ \psi - list assignment of a graph G G is a mapping L L which assigns to z ∈ V ∪ E z\in V\cup E a set L ⁡ ( z ) L(z) of ψ ⁡ ( z ) \psi(z) real numbers. Given a total list assignment L L , a proper L L -total weighting is a proper total weighting φ \varphi with φ ⁡ ( z ) ∈ L ⁡ ( z ) \varphi(z)\in L(z) for all z ∈ V ∪ E z\in V\cup E . We say G G is total weight ψ \psi - choosable ( ψ \psi - choosable for short) if for any ψ \psi -list assignment L L , there is a proper L L -total weighting of G G . We say G G is total weight ( k , k ′ ) (k,k^{\prime}) - choosable ( ( k , k ′ ) (k,k^{\prime}) -choosable for short) if G G is ψ \psi -total weight choosable, where ψ ⁡ ( v ) = k \psi(v)=k for v ∈ V ⁡ ( G ) v\in V(G) and ψ ⁡ ( e ) = k ′ \psi(e)=k^{\prime} for e ∈ E ⁡ ( G ) e\in E(G) . Wong and Zhu [ 8 ] proposed two Conjectures as follows: Conjecture 1.1 . [ 8 ] Every graph with no isolated edges is ( 1 , 3 ) (1,3) -choosable. Conjecture 1.2 . [ 8 ] Every graph is ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) -choosable. The permanent of an m × m m\times m real matrix A = [ a i ​ j ] A=[a_{ij}] , with i , j ∈ { 1 , 2 , … , m } i,j\in\{1,2,\ldots,m\} , is defined as per ⁡ ( A ) = ∑ σ ∏ i = 1 m a i ​ σ ​ ( i ) , {\rm per}(A)=\sum_{\sigma}\prod_{i=1}^{m}a_{i\sigma(i)}, where the summation takes over all permutations σ \sigma of { 1 , 2 , … , m } \{1,2,\ldots,m\} . The permanent index of a matrix A A , denoted by pind ⁡ ( A ) {\rm pind}(A) , is the minimum integer k k such that there exists a matrix A ′ A^{\prime} with per ⁡ ( A ′ ) ≠ 0 {\rm per}(A^{\prime})\neq 0 , each column of A ′ A^{\prime} is a column of A A and each column of A A occurs in A ′ A^{\prime} at most k k times. Let A G A_{G} be a matrix with rows indexed by the edges of G G and columns indexed by the vertices and edges of G G , where if e = ( u , v ) e=(u,v) (oriented from u u to v v ), then A G ​ [ e , y ] = { 1 if y = v , or y ≠ e is an edge incident to v , − 1 if y = u , or y ≠ e is an edge incident to u , 0 otherwise . \displaystyle A_{G}[e,y]=\begin{cases}1&\text{if $y=v$, or $y\neq e$ is an edge incident to $v$},\\ -1&\text{if $y=u$, or $y\neq e$ is an edge incident to $u$},\\ 0&\text{otherwise}.\\ \end{cases} and let B G B_{G} be the submatrix of A G A_{G} with those columns of A G A_{G} indexed by edges. An index function of G G is a mapping η \eta , and it assigns to every vertex or edge z z of G G a non-negative integer η \eta . If ∑ y ∈ V ⁡ ( G ) ∪ E ⁡ ( G ) η ⁡ ( z ) = | E ⁡ ( G ) | \sum_{y\in V(G)\cup E(G)}\eta(z)=|E(G)| , then the index function η \eta is valid . For an index function η \eta of G G , denote by A ( η ) A_{(\eta)} the matrix, each of its column is a column of A G A_{G} , and each column A G ​ ( z ) A_{G}(z) of A G A_{G} can appear up to η ⁡ ( z ) \eta(z) times in A ( η ) A_{(\eta)} . It is shown in [ 1 ] and [ 8 ] that G G is ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) -choosable if pind ⁡ ( A G ) = 1 {\rm pind}(A_{G})=1 , and G G is ( 1 , 3 ) (1,3) -choosable if pind ⁡ ( B G ) ≤ 2 {\rm pind}(B_{G})\leq 2 . Bartnicki et al. [ 2 ] and Wong et al. [ 8 ] proposed two Conjectures independently as follows: Conjecture 1.3 . [ 2 ] For any graph G G with no isolated edges, pind ⁡ ( B G ) ≤ 2 {\rm pind}(B_{G})\leq 2 . Conjecture 1.4 . [ 8 ] For any graph G G , pind ⁡ ( A G ) = 1 {\rm pind}(A_{G})=1 . The above two conjectures have received a lot of attention. However, they have not been solved yet, which can only be proved to be true for some special graphs. Recently, it was proved in [ 12 ] that every graph with no isolated edges is ( 1 , 5 ) (1,5) -choosable. Some special graphs are shown to be ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) -choosable, such as trees, complete graphs [ 8 ] , subcubic graphs, 2-trees, Halin graphs, grids [ 7 ] . Some special graphs are shown to be ( 1 , 3 ) (1,3) -choosable, such as complete graphs, complete bipartite graphs, trees without K 2 K_{2} [ 2 ] , Cartesian product of an even number of even cycles, of P n P_{n} and an even cycle, of two paths [ 9 ] . Wong and Zhu [ 8 ] showed that if a graph is ( k , k ′ ) (k,k^{\prime}) -choosable then it is ( k + 1 , k ′ ) (k+1,k^{\prime}) -choosable and ( k , k ′ + 1 ) (k,k^{\prime}+1) -choosable. Hence there is a natural problem as follows. Problem 1.5 . Characterizing graphs that are ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable. In response to the above problem, some results have been obtained. Wong et al. [ 10 ] proved that complete bipartite graphs without K 2 K_{2} are ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable; Chang et al. [ 3 ] proved that a tree with even number of edges is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable. In this paper, we focus on Problem 1.5 and Conjectures 1.3 and 1.4 , we show that some graphs are ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) -choosable as well as ( 1 , 3 ) (1,3) -choosable. The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. In Section 2, we determine a sufficient condition for a graph to be ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable. As applications, we show that an ( n , m ) (n,m) -graph is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable when m = n − 1 m=n-1 , n n and n + 1 n+1 . In Section 3, we prove that all ( n , m ) (n,m) -graphs are ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) -choosable as well as ( 1 , 3 ) (1,3) -choosable, where m = n m=n and n + 1 n+1 . In the final section, we prove that some graphs under some graph operations are ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) -choosable. 2 A solution to Problem 1.5 In this section, we will characterize a sufficient condition to answer Problem 1.5 . Chang et al. [ 3 ] gave an important result on ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable of graphs as follows. Lemma 2.1 . ( [ 3 ] ) If per ⁡ ( B G ) ≠ 0 {\rm per}(B_{G})\neq 0 . Then G G is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable. A Sachs graph is a simple graph such that each component is regular and has degree 1 or 2. In other words the components are single edges and cycles. Merris et al. [ 5 ] gave a formula for calculating the permanent of any graph G G : per ⁡ ( A ⁡ ( G ) ) = | ( − 1 ) n ​ ∑ H 2 k ⁡ ( H ) | , \displaystyle{\rm per}(A(G))=|(-1)^{n}\sum_{H}2^{k(H)}|, where the summation takes over all Sachs subgraphs H H of order n n in G G , and k ⁡ ( H ) k(H) is the number of cycles in H H . Theorem 2.2 . Let G G be a connected graph with m m edges. If the number of perfect matchings in the line graph L ⁡ ( G ) L(G) of G G is odd, then G G is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable. Proof. Replace − 1 -1 by 1 1 in B G B_{G} and the obtained matrix is just the adjacent matrix A ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( G ) ) A(L(G)) of L ⁡ ( G ) L(G) . It can be seen that per ⁡ ( B G ) ≡ per ⁡ ( A ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( G ) ) ) ​ ( mod ​ 2 ) {\rm per}(B_{G})\equiv{\rm per}(A(L(G)))({\rm mod}2) . According to formula ( 1 ) (1) , we get that per ⁡ ( A ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( G ) ) ) = | ( − 1 ) m ​ ∑ H 2 k ⁡ ( H ) | = M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( G ) ) + ∑ H ′ 2 k ⁡ ( H ′ ) , \displaystyle{\rm per}(A(L(G)))=|(-1)^{m}\sum_{H}2^{k(H)}|=M(L(G))+\sum_{H^{\prime}}2^{k(H^{\prime})}, where H ′ H^{\prime} denotes the Sachs subgraphs of m m vertices containing cycles of line graph L ⁡ ( G ) L(G) . Thus, per ⁡ ( A ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( G ) ) ) ≡ M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( G ) ) ​ ( mod ​ 2 ) . \displaystyle{\rm per}(A(L(G)))\equiv M(L(G))({\rm mod}~2). Furthermore, per ⁡ ( B G ) ≡ M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( G ) ) ​ ( mod ​ 2 ) . \displaystyle{\rm per}(B_{G})\equiv M(L(G))({\rm mod}~2). By Lemma 2.1 and the above equation, G G is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable if M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( G ) ) M(L(G)) is odd. ∎ As applications of Theorem 2.2 , we will show that some ( n , m ) (n,m) -graphs are ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable when m = n − 1 m=n-1 , n n and n + 1 n+1 as follows. Obviously, a connected ( n , m ) (n,m) -graph is a tree when m = n − 1 m=n-1 . Chang et al. [ 3 ] proved that a tree with even number of edges is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable. According to Theorem 2.2 , we can give a new proof. To achieve it, we first introduce some lemmas as follows. For any graph G G , let p ⁡ ( G ) p(G) be the number of components of G G which have an even number of edges. If G G is a forest, p ⁡ ( G ) p(G) and | V ⁡ ( G ) | |V(G)| have the same parity. Thus, if G G is a tree and | V ⁡ ( G ) | |V(G)| is odd, then p ⁡ ( G − v ) p(G-v) is even for all v ∈ V ⁡ ( G ) v\in V(G) . For any non-negative k k , denote by ( 2 ​ k ) !! = ( 2 ​ k ) ! k ! × 2 k (2k)!!=\frac{(2k)!}{k!\times 2^{k}} . Lemma 2.3 . ( [ 4 ] ) Let T T be a tree with V ⁡ ( T ) = { v 1 , v 2 , … , v n } V(T)=\{v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{n}\} , where n > 1 n>1 is odd. Then M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( T ) ) = ∏ i = 1 n p ⁡ ( T − v i ) !! . M(L(T))=\prod_{i=1}^{n}p(T-v_{i})!!. Lemma 2.4 . ( 2 ​ k ) ! k ! × 2 k = ( 2 ​ k − 1 ) × ( 2 ​ k − 3 ) × … × 3 × 1 \frac{(2k)!}{k!\times 2^{k}}=(2k-1)\times(2k-3)\times\ldots\times 3\times 1 , where k k is a non-negative integer. By Theorem 2.2 , Lemmas 2.3 and 2.4 , we can get a result as follows. Theorem 2.5 . ( [ 3 ] ) If T T is a tree with even number of edges. Then T T is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable. Next, we give a recursive expression for M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( G ) ) M(L(G)) . Let e e be any edge of G G with endvertices u u and v v . Let G ⁡ ( u , w ) G(u,w) be the graph obtained from G − e G-e by adding a new vertex w w and adding a new edge joining w w to u u . G ⁡ ( v , w ) G(v,w) is defined similarly. Lemma 2.6 . ( [ 4 ] ) Let G G be a graph, and let e = u ​ v e=uv be an edge of G G . Then M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( G ) ) = M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( G ⁡ ( u , w ) ) ) + M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( G ⁡ ( v , w ) ) ) . \displaystyle M(L(G))=M(L(G(u,w)))+M(L(G(v,w))). A vertex of degree one is called a leaf in a graph. A unicyclic graph is a connected graph containing exactly one cycle, the cycle denoted by C l C_{l} . Obviously, a connected ( n , m ) (n,m) -graph is unicyclic if and only if n = m n=m . The set of unicyclic graphs with n n vertices is denoted by 𝒰 n \mathscr{U}_{n} . For any graph U ∈ 𝒰 n U\in\mathscr{U}_{n} with V ⁡ ( C l ) = { v 1 , … , v l } ⊆ V ⁡ ( U ) V(C_{l})=\{v_{1},\ldots,v_{l}\}\subseteq V(U) , U U can be viewed as identifying v i v_{i} with any leaf of each of k i k_{i} trees for i ∈ { 1 , … , l } i\in\{1,\ldots,l\} , where k i k_{i} is a non-negative integer. Denote by k i 0 ( ≥ 2 ) k_{i}^{0}(\geq 2) and k i 1 ( ≥ 3 ) k_{i}^{1}(\geq 3) respectively the number of trees with even number of edges and odd number of edges in the k i k_{i} trees. Let s = ∑ i = 1 l k i 0 s=\sum_{i=1}^{l}k_{i}^{0} , 𝒰 1 \mathscr{U}_{1} be the subset of 𝒰 n \mathscr{U}_{n} such that s s is odd and 𝒰 2 \mathscr{U}_{2} be the subset of 𝒰 n \mathscr{U}_{n} such that s s is even. We denote the s s trees with even number of edges as T 1 , T 2 , … , T s T_{1},T_{2},\ldots,T_{s} , respectively. As we will consider the number of perfect matchings of line graphs, assume that n n is even and all the notation in this paragraph is followed in Theorem 2.7 and Lemma 2.8 . Theorem 2.7 . For any graph U ∈ 𝒰 1 U\in\mathscr{U}_{1} , U U is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable. Proof. By Lemma 2.6 , M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( U ) ) = M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( T 1 ) ) + M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( T 2 ) ) + … + M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( T s ) ) + M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( U ′ ) ) . \displaystyle M(L(U))=M(L(T_{1}))+M(L(T_{2}))+\ldots+M(L(T_{s}))+M(L(U^{\prime})). where e i e_{i} denotes the edge incident with T i T_{i} and v i v_{i} , U ′ = U − E ⁡ ( T 1 − e 1 ) − E ⁡ ( T 2 − e 2 ) − … − E ⁡ ( T s − e s ) U^{\prime}=U-E(T_{1}-e_{1})-E(T_{2}-e_{2})-\ldots-E(T_{s}-e_{s}) . According to the definition of 𝒰 1 \mathscr{U}_{1} , s s and m ⁡ ( T i − e i ) m(T_{i}-e_{i}) are odd, m ⁡ ( U ) m(U) is even. So m ⁡ ( U ′ ) = m ⁡ ( U ) − m ⁡ ( T 1 − e 1 ) − m ⁡ ( T 2 − e 2 ) − … − m ⁡ ( T s − e s ) m(U^{\prime})=m(U)-m(T_{1}-e_{1})-m(T_{2}-e_{2})-\ldots-m(T_{s}-e_{s}) is odd, then M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( U ′ ) ) = 0 M(L(U^{\prime}))=0 . By Theorem 2.5 , M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( T i ) ) M(L(T_{i})) is odd as m ⁡ ( T i ) m(T_{i}) is even. From the above argument and equation, we obtain that M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( U ) ) M(L(U)) is odd. Then U ∈ 𝒰 1 U\in\mathscr{U}_{1} is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable by Theorem 2.2 . ∎ Lemma 2.8 . Let U ∈ 𝒰 2 U\in\mathscr{U}_{2} . Then M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( U ) ) M(L(U)) is even. Proof. By Lemma 2.6 , M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( U ) ) \displaystyle M(L(U)) = \displaystyle= M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( T 1 ) ) + … + M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( T s ) ) + M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( U ′ ) ) \displaystyle M(L(T_{1}))+\ldots+M(L(T_{s}))+M(L(U^{\prime})) = \displaystyle= M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( T 1 ) ) + … + M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( T s ) ) + M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( U ′ ​ ( u , w ) ) ) + M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( U ′ ​ ( v , w ) ) ) , \displaystyle M(L(T_{1}))+\ldots+M(L(T_{s}))+M(L(U^{\prime}(u,w)))+M(L(U^{\prime}(v,w))), where e i e_{i} denotes the edge incident with T i T_{i} and v i v_{i} , U ′ = U − E ⁡ ( T 1 − e 1 ) − E ⁡ ( T 2 − e 2 ) − … − E ⁡ ( T s − e s ) U^{\prime}=U-E(T_{1}-e_{1})-E(T_{2}-e_{2})-\ldots-E(T_{s}-e_{s}) , e = u ​ v e=uv denotes any edge of C l C_{l} in U ′ U^{\prime} and U ′ ​ ( x , w ) U^{\prime}(x,w) is the graph obtained from G − e G-e by adding a new vertex w w and adding a new edge w ​ x wx for x ∈ { u , v } x\in\{u,v\} . By the above definition of 𝒰 2 \mathscr{U}_{2} , m ⁡ ( T i − e i ) m(T_{i}-e_{i}) is odd, s s and m ⁡ ( U ) m(U) are even. So, m ⁡ ( U ′ ) = m ⁡ ( U ) − m ⁡ ( T 1 − e 1 ) − m ⁡ ( T 2 − e 2 ) − … − m ⁡ ( T s − e s ) m(U^{\prime})=m(U)-m(T_{1}-e_{1})-m(T_{2}-e_{2})-\ldots-m(T_{s}-e_{s}) is even. Hence m ​ ( U ′ ​ ( u , w ) ) m(U^{\prime}(u,w)) and m ​ ( U ′ ​ ( v , w ) ) m(U^{\prime}(v,w)) are even, and U ′ ​ ( u , w ) , U ′ ​ ( v , w ) U^{\prime}(u,w),U^{\prime}(v,w) are trees. By Theorem 2.5 , M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( U ′ ​ ( u , w ) ) ) M(L(U^{\prime}(u,w))) and M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( U ′ ​ ( v , w ) ) ) M(L(U^{\prime}(v,w))) are odd. Since m ⁡ ( T i ) m(T_{i}) is even, by Theorem 2.5 , we have that M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( T i ) ) M(L(T_{i})) is odd. From the above argument and equation, we obtain that M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( U ) ) M(L(U)) is even. ∎ A connected ( n , m ) (n,m) -graph containing two or three cycles is called a bicyclic graph if m = n + 1 m=n+1 . Let ℬ n \mathscr{B}_{n} be the set of all bicyclic graphs with n n vertices. By the structure of bicyclic graphs, it is known that ℬ n \mathscr{B}_{n} consists of three types of graphs: the first type, denoted by ℬ n 1 ​ ( p , q ) \mathscr{B}_{n}^{1}(p,q) , is the set of graphs each of which contains B 1 ​ ( p , q ) B_{1}(p,q) as a vertex-induced subgraph; the second type, denoted by ℬ n 2 ​ ( p , q , r ) \mathscr{B}_{n}^{2}(p,q,r) , is the set of graphs each of which contains B 2 ​ ( p , q , r ) B_{2}(p,q,r) as a vertex-induced subgraph; the third type, denoted by ℬ n 3 ​ ( p , q , r ) \mathscr{B}_{n}^{3}(p,q,r) , is the set of graphs each of which contains B 3 ​ ( p , q , r ) B_{3}(p,q,r) as a vertex-induced subgraph (see Figure 1 ). Obviously, ℬ n = ℬ n 1 ​ ( p , q ) ∪ ℬ n 2 ​ ( p , q , r ) ∪ ℬ n 3 ​ ( p , q , r ) \mathscr{B}_{n}=\mathscr{B}_{n}^{1}(p,q)\cup\mathscr{B}_{n}^{2}(p,q,r)\cup\mathscr{B}_{n}^{3}(p,q,r) . Figure 1: Bicyclic graphs B 1 ​ ( p , q ) B_{1}(p,q) , B 2 ​ ( p , q , r ) B_{2}(p,q,r) and B 3 ​ ( p , q , r ) B_{3}(p,q,r) Let C p C_{p} and C q C_{q} denote the induced cycles of any bicyclic graph in ℬ n 1 ​ ( p , q ) \mathscr{B}_{n}^{1}(p,q) . For any graph B ∈ ℬ n 1 ​ ( p , q ) B\in\mathscr{B}_{n}^{1}(p,q) with V ⁡ ( B 1 ​ ( p , q ) ) = { v 0 , v 1 , … , v p + q − 1 } ⊆ V ⁡ ( B ) V(B_{1}(p,q))=\{v_{0},v_{1},\ldots,v_{p+q-1}\}\subseteq V(B) , B B can be viewed as identifying v i v_{i} with any leaf of each of k i k_{i} trees for i ∈ { 1 , … , p + q − 1 } i\in\{1,\ldots,p+q-1\} , where k i k_{i} is a non-negative integer. Denote by k i 0 ( ≥ 2 ) k_{i}^{0}(\geq 2) and k i 1 ( ≥ 3 ) k_{i}^{1}(\geq 3) respectively the number of trees with even number of edges and odd number of edges in the k i k_{i} trees. Let s 1 = ∑ i k i 0 s_{1}=\sum_{i}k_{i}^{0} , where the summation takes over all vertices of C p C_{p} or C q C_{q} , ℬ 1 ⊂ ℬ n 1 ​ ( p , q ) \mathscr{B}_{1}\subset\mathscr{B}_{n}^{1}(p,q) such that each graph in ℬ 1 \mathscr{B}_{1} contains even number of edges and s 1 s_{1} is odd or even. As we will consider the number of perfect matchings of line graphs, assume that n n is odd and all the notation in this paragraph and Figure 1 is followed in Lemmas 2.9 , 2.10 , 2.11 , 2.12 and Theorem 2.13 . Lemma 2.9 . Let B ∈ ℬ 1 B\in\mathscr{B}_{1} be a bicyclic graph. Then B B is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable. Proof. Let e = u ​ v 1 e=uv_{1} as in Figure 1 . By Lemma 2.6 , M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ) ) = M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( u , w ) ) ) + M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( v 1 , w ) ) ) . \displaystyle M(L(B))=M(L(B(u,w)))+M(L(B(v_{1},w))). Clearly, B ⁡ ( u , w ) , B ⁡ ( v 1 , w ) ∈ 𝒰 n B(u,w),