, w ) , B ⁡ ( z , w ) ∈ 𝒰 n B(u,w),B(z,w)\in\mathscr{U}_{n} . According to the definition of ℬ 3 \mathscr{B}_{3} , m ⁡ ( B ) m(B) is even. We consider two cases as follows. Case 1: m ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( u , w ) ) m(B(u,w)) is even and m ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( z , w ) ) m(B(z,w)) is odd. By the above definition, we have ∑ i k i 0 = s 1 \sum_{i}k_{i}^{0}=s_{1} is odd, where the summation takes over all vertices of C p C_{p} . Then M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( z , w ) ) ) = 0 M(L(B(z,w)))=0 . By Theorem 2.7 , M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( u , w ) ) ) M(L(B(u,w))) is odd. It follows that M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ) ) M(L(B)) is odd by the above equation and B ∈ ℬ 3 B\in\mathscr{B}_{3} is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable by Theorem 2.2 . Case 2: m ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( u , w ) ) m(B(u,w)) is odd and m ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( z , w ) ) m(B(z,w)) is even. By the above definition, we have ∑ i k i 0 = s 1 \sum_{i}k_{i}^{0}=s_{1} is even, where the summation takes over all vertices of C q C_{q} . Then M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( u , w ) ) ) = 0 M(L(B(u,w)))=0 . By Theorem 2.7 , M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( z , w ) ) ) M(L(B(z,w))) is odd. It follows that M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ) ) M(L(B)) is odd by the above equation and B ∈ ℬ 3 B\in\mathscr{B}_{3} is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable by Theorem 2.2 . ∎ Let ℬ 4 ⊂ ℬ n 3 ​ ( p , q , r ) \mathscr{B}_{4}\subset\mathscr{B}_{n}^{3}(p,q,r) be the set of all graphs obtained by identifying every vertex v i v_{i} of B 3 ​ ( p , q , r ) B_{3}(p,q,r) with any leaf of each of the k i k_{i} trees, where k i = k i 0 + k i 1 k_{i}=k_{i}^{0}+k_{i}^{1} (the number of trees with even number of edges ( ≥ 2 ) (\geq 2) is denoted by k i 0 k_{i}^{0} , the number of trees with odd number of edges ( ≥ 3 ) (\geq 3) is denoted by k i 1 k_{i}^{1} ) and ∑ i k i 0 = s 1 \sum_{i}k_{i}^{0}=s_{1} is odd or even, where the summation takes over all vertices of P p P_{p} , P q P_{q} and u , v u,v . Because we shall think about the number of perfect matchings of line graph, n n is assumed to be odd. Lemma 2.12 . Let B ∈ ℬ 4 B\in\mathscr{B}_{4} be a bicyclic graph. Then B B is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable. Proof. Let e = u ​ y e=uy . By Lemma 2.6 , M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ) ) = M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( u , w ) ) ) + M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( y , w ) ) ) . \displaystyle M(L(B))=M(L(B(u,w)))+M(L(B(y,w))). Clearly, B ⁡ ( u , w ) , B ⁡ ( y , w ) ∈ 𝒰 n B(u,w),B(y,w)\in\mathscr{U}_{n} . By the definition of ℬ 4 \mathscr{B}_{4} , m ⁡ ( B ) m(B) is even. Then the number of edges of B ⁡ ( u , w ) B(u,w) and B ⁡ ( y , w ) B(y,w) are both even. Set s = x ′ s=x^{\prime} for B ⁡ ( u , w ) B(u,w) and s = y ′ s=y^{\prime} for B ⁡ ( y , w ) B(y,w) . Let B ′ B^{\prime} be a subgraph of B B induced by P r P_{r} , edges u ​ y , v ​ x uy,vx and the trees hanging on all vertices of P r P_{r} . We consider two cases as follows. Case 1: m ⁡ ( B ′ ) m(B^{\prime}) is even. If s 1 s_{1} is odd, then x ′ = s 1 x^{\prime}=s_{1} and y ′ = s 1 + 1 y^{\prime}=s_{1}+1 . Then M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( u , w ) ) ) M(L(B(u,w))) is odd by Theorem 2.7 and M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( y , w ) ) ) M(L(B(y,w))) is even by Lemma 2.8 . It follows that M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ) ) M(L(B)) is odd by the above equation and B ∈ ℬ 4 B\in\mathscr{B}_{4} is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable by Theorem 2.2 . If s 1 s_{1} is even, then x ′ = s 1 x^{\prime}=s_{1} and y ′ = s 1 + 1 y^{\prime}=s_{1}+1 . Hence, M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( u , w ) ) ) M(L(B(u,w))) is even by Lemma 2.8 and M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( y , w ) ) ) M(L(B(y,w))) is odd by Theorem 2.7 . It follows that M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ) ) M(L(B)) is odd by the above equation ( 7 ) (7) and B ∈ ℬ 4 B\in\mathscr{B}_{4} is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable by Theorem 2.2 . Case 2: m ⁡ ( B ′ ) m(B^{\prime}) is odd. If s 1 s_{1} is odd, then x ′ = s 1 + 1 x^{\prime}=s_{1}+1 and y ′ = s 1 y^{\prime}=s_{1} . Hence, M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( u , w ) ) ) M(L(B(u,w))) is even by Lemma 2.8 . According to Theorem 2.7 , we have M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( y , w ) ) ) M(L(B(y,w))) is odd. It follows that M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ) ) M(L(B)) is odd by the above equation ( 7 ) (7) and B ∈ ℬ 4 B\in\mathscr{B}_{4} is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable by Theorem 2.2 . If s 1 s_{1} is even,then x ′ = s 1 + 1 x^{\prime}=s_{1}+1 and y ′ = s 1 y^{\prime}=s_{1} . Hence, M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( u , w ) ) ) M(L(B(u,w))) is odd by Theorem 2.7 and M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ⁡ ( y , w ) ) ) M(L(B(y,w))) is even by Lemma 2.8 . It follows that M ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( B ) ) M(L(B)) is odd by the above equation and B ∈ ℬ 4 B\in\mathscr{B}_{4} is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable by Theorem 2.2 . ∎ According to Lemmas 2.9 , 2.10 , 2.11 and 2.12 , we obtain the following result in this section. Theorem 2.13 . Let B ∈ ℬ 1 ∪ ℬ 2 ∪ ℬ 3 ∪ ℬ 4 B\in\mathscr{B}_{1}\cup\mathscr{B}_{2}\cup\mathscr{B}_{3}\cup\mathscr{B}_{4} be a bicyclic graph. Then B B is ( 1 , 2 ) (1,2) -choosable. 3 Total weight choosability of ( n , m ) (n,m) -graphs when m = n m=n and n + 1 n+1 In this section, we will show that all ( n , m ) (n,m) -graphs are ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) -choosable and ( 1 , 3 ) (1,3) -choosable, where m = n m=n and n + 1 n+1 . Obviously, ( n , m ) (n,m) -graphs are unicyclic graphs when m = n m=n ; ( n , m ) (n,m) -graphs are bicyclic graphs when m = n + 1 m=n+1 . A sink in a digraph is a vertex of outdegree zero. Before the proof of main theorems, we present some lemmas as follows. Lemma 3.1 . ( [ 7 ] ) Let an index function η \eta be non-singular if there is a valid index function η ′ ≤ η \eta^{\prime}\leq\eta with per ⁡ ( A G ​ ( η ′ ) ) ≠ 0 {\rm per}(A_{G}(\eta^{\prime}))\neq 0 . Suppose G G is a graph, η \eta is an index function of G G with η ⁡ ( e ) = 1 \eta(e)=1 for all edges e e , and X X is a subset of V ⁡ ( G ) V(G) . Let G ′ = G − E ⁡ [ X ] G^{\prime}=G-E[X] be obtained from G G by deleting edges in G ⁡ [ X ] G[X] . Let D D be an acyclic orientation of G ′ G^{\prime} , in which each vertex v ∈ X v\in X is a sink. Assume that D ′ D^{\prime} is a sub-digraph of D D such that for all v ∈ V ⁡ ( D ) v\in V(D) : η ⁡ ( v ) + 2 ​ d D ′ − ​ ( v ) − d D − ​ ( v ) ≥ d D ′ + ​ ( v ) . \displaystyle\eta(v)+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v). Let η ′ \eta^{\prime} be the index function defined as η ′ ​ ( e ) = 1 \eta^{\prime}(e)=1 for every edge e e of G ⁡ [ X ] G[X] and η ′ ​ ( v ) = η ⁡ ( v ) + 2 ​ d D ′ − ​ ( v ) − d D − ​ ( v ) \eta^{\prime}(v)=\eta(v)+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v) for v ∈ X v\in X . If η ′ \eta^{\prime} is a non-singular index function for G ⁡ [ X ] G[X] , then η \eta is a non-singular index function for G G . Lemma 3.2 . ( [ 8 ] ) Suppose G G is obtained from a graph G ′ G^{\prime} by adding one vertex v v and one edge e = u ​ v e=uv , where u u is a vertex of G ′ G^{\prime} . If pind ⁡ ( A G ′ ) = 1 {\rm pind}(A_{G^{\prime}})=1 , then pind ⁡ ( A G ) = 1 {\rm pind}(A_{G})=1 . If G ′ G^{\prime} is ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) -choosable, then G G is ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) -choosable. Lemma 3.3 . ( [ 2 ] ) If T T is a tree with at least two edges, then pind ⁡ ( B T ) ≤ 2 {\rm pind}(B_{T})\leq 2 . Hence T T is ( 1 , 3 ) (1,3) -choosable. A hanging edge of a graph G G is an edge e = u ​ v e=uv of G G such that d G ​ ( v ) = 1 d_{G}(v)=1 and d G ​ ( u ) = 2 d_{G}(u)=2 or 3. Lemma 3.4 . ( [ 8 ] ) Let G G be a graph containing a hanging edge e = u ​ v e=uv and G ′ = G − { u , v } G^{\prime}=G-\{u,v\} . If pind ⁡ ( B G ′ ) ≤ 2 {\rm pind}(B_{G^{\prime}})\leq 2 , then pind ⁡ ( B G ) ≤ 2 {\rm pind}(B_{G})\leq 2 . Lemma 3.5 . ( [ 11 ] ) Let G ′ G^{\prime} be obtained from a graph G G by adding two new vertices u , v u,v and two new edges e 1 = u ​ v , e 2 = u ​ w e_{1}=uv,e_{2}=uw , where w ∈ V ⁡ ( G ) w\in V(G) . Then pind ⁡ ( B G ′ ) ≤ pind ⁡ ( B G ) {\rm pind}(B_{G^{\prime}})\leq{\rm pind}(B_{G}) . Lemma 3.6 . ( [ 2 ] ) Let G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) be a graph such that pind ⁡ ( B G ) ≤ 2 {\rm pind}(B_{G})\leq 2 and U U be a nonempty subset of V ⁡ ( G ) V(G) . Denote by F F the graph obtained by adding two new vertices u , v u,v to G G and joining them to each vertex of U U . Then pind ⁡ ( B F ) ≤ 2 {\rm pind}(B_{F})\leq 2 . A thread in a graph G G is a path P = ( v 1 , v 2 , … , v k ) P=(v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{k}) in G G such that d G ​ ( v i ) = 2 d_{G}(v_{i})=2 for i = 2 , 3 , … , k − 1 i=2,3,\ldots,k-1 . The vertices v 1 , v k v_{1},v_{k} need not to be distinct. If we need to specify the two end vertices of a thread, then we say P P is a ( v 1 − v k ) (v_{1}-v_{k}) -thread. By deleting a thread P = ( v 1 , v 2 , … , v k ) P=(v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{k}) from G G , we mean deleting the vertices v 2 , v 3 , … , v k − 1 v_{2},v_{3},\ldots,v_{k-1} (and hence edges incident to them). The length of a thread is the number of edges in it. The notations defined in section 2 are followed in this section. Lemma 3.7 . ( [ 8 ] ) Let G ′ G^{\prime} be obtained from a graph G G by deleting a thread of length 4. If pind ⁡ ( B G ′ ) ≤ 2 {\rm pind}(B_{G^{\prime}})\leq 2 , then pind ⁡ ( B G ) ≤ 2 {\rm pind}(B_{G})\leq 2 . Theorem 3.8 . Let U ∈ 𝒰 n U\in\mathscr{U}_{n} , then pind ⁡ ( A U ) = 1 {\rm pind}(A_{U})=1 . Hence U U is ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) -choosable. Proof. According to Lemma 3.2 , it is sufficient to prove this Theorem holds for the unique cycle C l C_{l} in U U . First we construct an acyclic orientation of C l C_{l} as follows: orient the edges v i ​ v i + 1 ​ ( i = 1 , 2 , … , l − 1 ) v_{i}v_{i+1}(i=1,2,\ldots,l-1) from v i v_{i} to v i + 1 v_{i+1} and orient the edge v 1 ​ v l v_{1}v_{l} from v 1 v_{1} to v l v_{l} . The resulting digraph is denoted by D D and v l v_{l} is a sink vertex in D D obviously. Let D ′ D^{\prime} be a sub-digraph of D D consisting of the edge v 1 ​ v l v_{1}v_{l} . Let η ≡ 1 \eta\equiv 1 be a constant function, X = { v l } X=\{v_{l}\} and η ′ ​ ( v l ) = 0 \eta^{\prime}(v_{l})=0 be an index function of U ⁡ [ X ] U[X] . Because there exist a valid index function η ′′ ≤ η ′ \eta^{\prime\prime}\leq\eta^{\prime} with per ⁡ ( A U ⁡ [ X ] ​ ( η ′′ ) ) ≠ 0 {\rm per}(A_{U[X]}(\eta^{\prime\prime}))\neq 0 . Then η ′ \eta^{\prime} is a non-singular index function of U ⁡ [ X ] U[X] . To prove that pind ⁡ ( A U ) = 1 {\rm pind}(A_{U})=1 , i.e., η \eta is a non-singular index function of U U , is suffices, by Lemma