e proof of the case i = 2 i=2 is similar to i = 1 i=1 and is thus omitted. By repeating the above process, we can prove the above Theorem holds for i = 3 , 4 , … , l i=3,4,\ldots,l . Case 3: U U is a graph obtained by identifying vertex v i v_{i} of C l C_{l} with a vertex of a tree T s i T_{s_{i}} , where 1 ≤ i ≤ l 1\leq i\leq l and at least one T s i T_{s_{i}} is not a star. We prove the Theorem by induction on m ′ = | ⋃ i = 1 l E ⁡ ( T s i ) | m^{\prime}=|\bigcup\limits_{i=1}^{l}E(T_{s_{i}})| . If m ′ = 1 m^{\prime}=1 , then pind ⁡ ( B U ) ≤ 2 {\rm pind}(B_{U})\leq 2 according to Case 2 2 . Assume that the above Theorem holds for the number of edges in the hanging trees less than m ′ ( ≥ 2 ) m^{\prime}(\geq 2) . Consider the case that | ⋃ i = 1 l E ⁡ ( T s i ) | = m ′ |\bigcup\limits_{i=1}^{l}E(T_{s_{i}})|=m^{\prime} . By induction hypothesis, the above Theorem holds for m ′ − 2 m^{\prime}-2 and pind ⁡ ( B U ) ≤ 2 {\rm pind}(B_{U})\leq 2 by Lemmas 3.5 and 3.6 . Hence U U is ( 1 , 3 ) (1,3) -choosable. ∎ Theorem 3.10 . Let B ∈ ℬ n B\in\mathscr{B}_{n} , then pind ⁡ ( A B ) = 1 {\rm pind}(A_{B})=1 . Hence B B is ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) -choosable. Proof. According to Lemma 3.2 , we only need to prove this Theorem holds for B 1 ​ ( p , q ) B_{1}(p,q) , B 2 ​ ( p , q , r ) B_{2}(p,q,\\ r) , B 3 ​ ( p , q , r ) B_{3}(p,q,r) . We consider three cases. First we construct an acyclic orientation of B 1 ​ ( p , q ) B_{1}(p,q) as follows: For C p C_{p} , except for the clockwise orientation of edge v 1 ​ u v_{1}u , all the other edges are oriented anticlockwise; For C q C_{q} , except for the anticlockwise orientation of edge v 2 ​ u v_{2}u , all the other edges are oriented clockwise. The resulting digraph is denoted by D D . Let D ′ D^{\prime} be a sub-digraph of D D consisting of the edges v 1 ​ u , v 2 ​ u v_{1}u,v_{2}u . It is easy to see that u u is a sink of D D . Let η ≡ 1 \eta\equiv 1 be a constant function, X = { u } X=\{u\} and η ′ ​ ( u ) = 0 \eta^{\prime}(u)=0 be an ind