R ⁡ ( G ) R(G) is obtained from G G by adding | E ⁡ ( G ) | |E(G)| new vertices and joining each of them to the endvertices of exactly one edge in E ⁡ ( G ) E(G) . • Q ⁡ ( G ) Q(G) : Q ⁡ ( G ) Q(G) is obtained from G G by inserting a new vertex into each edge of G G , then joining those pairs of new vertices on adjacent edges of G G with edges. In order to obtain the main theorems, we present a lemma and a theorem as follows. Lemma 4.1 . ( [ 11 ] ) Assume that A A is an n × m n\times m matrix and L L is an n × n n\times n matrix whose columns are linear combinations of the columns of A A . Let the j j th column of A A be present in n j n_{j} such linear combinations (with non-zero coefficients). Then there is an index function η \eta : { 1 , 2 , … , m } → { 0 , 1 , … } \{1,2,\ldots,m\}\rightarrow\{0,1,\ldots\} such that η ⁡ ( j ) ≤ n j \eta(j)\leq n_{j} and per ⁡ ( A ⁡ ( η ) ) ≠ 0 {\rm per}(A{(\eta)})\neq 0 . Theorem 4.2 . Let G G be a connected graph, and G ′ G^{\prime} be a graph obtained by identifying a vertex of G G with a vertex of K 3 K_{3} . If pind ⁡ ( A G ) = 1 {\rm pind}(A_{G})=1 , then pind ⁡ ( A G ′ ) = 1 {\rm pind}(A_{G^{\prime}})=1 and hence G ′ G^{\prime} is ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) -choosable. Proof. Assume that G G is a graph with n n vertices, m m edges and there exists an orientation of G G such that pind ⁡ ( A G ) = 1 {\rm pind}(A_{G})=1 . By the definition of A G A_{G} , A G A_{G} is an m × ( m + n ) m\times(m+n) matrix. Therefore, according to the definition of permanent index of A G A_{G} and the assumption, A G A_{G} has an m × m m\times m submatrix B ′ B^{\prime} such that per ⁡ ( B ′ ) ≠ 0 {\rm per}(B^{\prime})\neq 0 and each column of B ′ B^{\prime} is a column of A G A_{G} , each column of A G A_{G} occurs in B ′ B^{\prime} at most once. For K 3 K_{3} , the vertices are v 1 , v 2 , v 3 v_{1},v_{2},v_{3} , and the edges are e i ​ j = v i ​ v j e_{ij}=v_{i}v_{j} for 1 ≤ j < i ≤ 3 1\leq j<i\leq 3 . According to the assumption of G ′ G^{\prime} , the