le. Proof. We prove this theorem by induction on n n . n = 1 n=1 is trivial. By Lemma 3.2 , this Theorem holds for n = 2 n=2 . Assume that the theorem holds for n = k − 1 n=k-1 . Now, we consider the case n = k n=k . Let G ′′ G^{\prime\prime} be a graph obtained by identifying a vertex of G G and a vertex of K k − 1 K_{k-1} , where V ⁡ ( K k − 1 ) = { v 1 , v 2 , … ​ v k − 1 } V(K_{k-1})=\{v_{1},v_{2},\ldots v_{k-1}\} . By induction, we can obtain that G ′′ G^{\prime\prime} is ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) -choosable. Hence, there exists a ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) -total-weight-list assignment of G ′′ G^{\prime\prime} denoted by L L such that there exists a proper L L -total-weighting of G ′′ G^{\prime\prime} . Next, based on the proper L L -total weighting of G ′′ G^{\prime\prime} , we need to find a proper L ′ L^{\prime} -total weighting of G ′ G^{\prime} . Checking the structure of G ′ G^{\prime} , G ′ G^{\prime} be a graph obtained from G ′′ G^{\prime\prime} by adding a new vertex v k v_{k} and some new edges which joining v k v_{k} to all vertices in V ⁡ ( K k − 1 ) V(K_{k-1}) . For convenience, denote by e 1 = v 1 v k , e 2 = v 2 v k , … , e k − 1 = v k − 1 v k e_{1}=v_{1}v_{k},e_{2}=v_{2}v_{k},\ldots,e_{k-1}=v_{k-1}v_{k} the new edges of G ′ G^{\prime} . Let L ′ L^{\prime} be a ( 2 , 2 ) (2,2) -total-list assignment of G ′ G^{\prime} , defined as follows: For L ′ ​ ( e 1 ) , L ′ ​ ( e 2 ) , … ​ L ′ ​ ( e k − 1 ) L^{\prime}(e_{1}),L^{\prime}(e_{2}),\ldots L^{\prime}(e_{k-1}) , choose w 1 ∈ L ′ ( e 1 ) , w 2 ∈ L ′ ( e 2 ) , … , w k − 1 ∈ L ′ ( e k − 1 ) w_{1}\in L^{\prime}(e_{1}),w_{2}\in L^{\prime}(e_{2}),\ldots,w_{k-1}\in L^{\prime}(e_{k-1}) ; L ′ ​ ( z ) = L ​ ( z ) L^{\prime}(z)=L(z) if z ∉ { v 1 , v 2 , … ​ v k , e 1 , e 2 , … ​ e k − 1 } z\notin\{v_{1},v_{2},\ldots v_{k},e_{1},e_{2},\ldots e_{k-1}\} ; L ′ ( v 1 ) = L ( v 1 ) − w 1 , L ′ ( v 2 ) = L ( v 2 ) − w 2 , … , L ′ ( v k − 1 ) = L ( v k − 1 ) − w k − 1 L^{\prime}(v_{1})=L(v_{1})-w_{1},L^{\prime}(v_{2})=L(v_{2})-w_{2},\ld